1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,644
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,702 --> 00:00:09,805
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,805 --> 00:00:10,510
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:13,329
Welcome to Unit 11: Magnetism.

5
00:00:14,388 --> 00:00:20,023
Maglev trains have no wheels: powerful magnets lift them above the track and push them forward.

6
00:00:20,023 --> 00:00:25,013
They use less energy than electric trains and emit far less carbon than diesel ones.

7
00:00:25,013 --> 00:00:28,007
How else can magnets help us live more sustainably?

8
00:00:29,065 --> 00:00:31,883
Every magnet has a North pole and a South pole.

9
00:00:31,883 --> 00:00:36,051
Bring two magnets together: unlike poles attract, and like poles repel.

10
00:00:37,109 --> 00:00:42,216
Materials attracted to magnets are magnetic materials: iron, nickel, cobalt and steel.

11
00:00:42,216 --> 00:00:46,971
Wood, plastic, glass, and even metals like copper and aluminium are non-magnetic.

12
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:53,959
Permanent magnets keep their magnetism, although heating, hammering or dropping them can destroy it.

13
00:00:53,959 --> 00:00:58,831
Temporary magnets, made of soft iron, are magnetic only while in a magnetic field.

14
00:00:58,831 --> 00:01:01,531
And electromagnets can be switched on and off.

15
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:08,166
A strong magnet is lowered over five objects: an iron nail, a steel paper clip, a copper coin,

16
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:14,389
an aluminium can and a plastic spoon. Predict: which objects will jump up to the magnet? Pause and decide.

17
00:01:15,447 --> 00:01:18,735
Watch. The magnet moves slowly down towards the objects.

18
00:01:19,793 --> 00:01:22,904
Only the iron nail and the steel paper clip jump up.

19
00:01:22,904 --> 00:01:27,307
The copper coin, the aluminium can and the plastic spoon stay on the table.

20
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:33,825
Explain what you saw. Only magnetic materials are attracted: iron, steel, nickel and cobalt.

21
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:39,401
Copper and aluminium are metals, but they are not magnetic, and plastic is not a metal at all.

22
00:01:39,401 --> 00:01:44,626
So 'all metals are magnetic' is a common mistake. Pause and explain it in your own words.

23
00:01:45,685 --> 00:01:50,674
Around every magnet is an invisible region where its force acts: the magnetic field.

24
00:01:50,674 --> 00:01:55,546
Sprinkle iron filings around a bar magnet, and they line up along the field lines.

25
00:01:55,546 --> 00:02:01,123
The lines go from the North pole to the South pole, and they're closest together at the poles,

26
00:02:01,123 --> 00:02:02,825
where the field is strongest.

27
00:02:03,884 --> 00:02:08,756
The field gets weaker further from the magnet. Move a magnet towards a paper clip,

28
00:02:08,756 --> 00:02:13,628
and at a certain distance the force becomes strong enough to pull the clip across.

29
00:02:13,628 --> 00:02:15,624
Magnetic force acts at a distance!

30
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:19,735
When two magnets come close, their fields interact.

31
00:02:19,735 --> 00:02:23,962
Opposite poles: the field lines join up, and the magnets pull together.

32
00:02:23,962 --> 00:02:27,660
Like poles: the field lines push apart, and the magnets repel.

33
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:31,241
Between them is a neutral point, where the forces cancel out.

34
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:38,169
Earth has a magnetic field too, shaped like a bar magnet's, although there's no real magnet inside!

35
00:02:38,169 --> 00:02:43,276
Moving molten metal in the outer core creates electric currents that produce the field.

36
00:02:44,335 --> 00:02:48,503
Earth's field makes compass needles point north, helps birds navigate,

37
00:02:48,503 --> 00:02:51,907
and protects us from fast charged particles from the Sun.

38
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:56,897
Its magnetic poles aren't quite at the geographic poles, so maps show the difference.
